Found four blocking issues in Phase 13.

- **[P1] Provider version and resolved settings are absent from workflow cache fingerprints.** The scheduler fingerprints raw node configuration before adapters resolve the canonical provider and saved settings ([scheduler.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:686)). Resolution happens afterward inside the adapters ([story.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/story.py:44), [scenes.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/scenes.py:44)). Consequently, changing provider settings or upgrading a provider version can reuse an artifact generated with the previous provider configuration. This directly misses 13.3’s requirement that fingerprints include canonical provider ID/version and normalized options.

- **[P1] Legacy generation pages ignore the authoritative provider selection.** When `provider_id` is absent, both route resolvers jump directly to `DomainSpec.default_provider` ([story/routes.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/story/routes.py:254), [build_scene_blueprints/routes.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/build_scene_blueprints/routes.py:273)) and never read `domains.<domain>.selected_provider`. The existing frontend callers omit `provider_id`, so selecting `random_template` globally still makes the Story page invoke Gemini; an alternate selected scene provider would likewise be ignored. This violates the frozen request → settings → domain-default precedence.

- **[P1] Production dispatch bypasses the standard provider invocation boundary and typed contracts.** Both workflow adapters call `provider.generate(...)` directly ([story.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/story.py:55), [scenes.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/scenes.py:51)). The `ScriptRequest`/`SceneBlueprintRequest`, result-model validation, provenance, artifact checks, cancellation, and standard exception boundary are therefore not exercised in production. A malformed third-party provider result can reach ports or fail as an incidental `KeyError`; worse, the legacy story route returns unexpected exception text verbatim ([routes.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/story/routes.py:331)), potentially exposing paths or provider response details.

- **[P2] Saved provider settings overwrite explicit request configuration, while aliases miss canonical settings.** The adapters apply `configuration.update(provider_run_options(...))` after building explicit configuration ([story.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/story.py:52), [scenes.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/scenes.py:48)). Thus a saved `webhook_url` can overwrite a node/request-level URL, reversing “request wins.” They also pass the original selection to settings lookup rather than the canonical ID, so a saved workflow using the permanent `builtin` alias resolves the provider correctly but looks for settings under `per_provider.builtin` instead of `gemini`/`n8n`.

Verification completed successfully: 719 backend tests, 280 frontend tests, production build, and generated-document drift check all pass. The gaps above are not covered by the current suite. No files were changed.
